(CHAPTER I. Down the Rabbit-Hole) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Alice was beginning to get very tired of sitting by her sister on the bank,) Tj T*
(and of having nothing to do: once or twice she had peeped into the book her) Tj T*
(sister was reading, but it had no pictures or conversations in it, 'and what) Tj T*
(is the use of a book,' thought Alice 'without pictures or conversations?') Tj T*
() Tj T*
(So she was considering in her own mind \(as well as she could, for the hot day) Tj T*
(made her feel very sleepy and stupid\), whether the pleasure of making a) Tj T*
(daisy-chain would be worth the trouble of getting up and picking the daisies,) Tj T*
(when suddenly a White Rabbit with pink eyes ran close by her.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There was nothing so very remarkable in that; nor did Alice think it so very) Tj T*
(much out of the way to hear the Rabbit say to itself, 'Oh dear! Oh dear! I) Tj T*
(shall be late!' \(when she thought it over afterwards, it occurred to her that) Tj T*
(she ought to have wondered at this, but at the time it all seemed quite) Tj T*
(natural\); but when the Rabbit actually took a watch out of its) Tj T*
(waistcoat-pocket, and looked at it, and then hurried on, Alice started to her) Tj T*
(feet, for it flashed across her mind that she had never before seen a rabbit) Tj T*
(with either a waistcoat-pocket, or a watch to take out of it, and burning with) Tj T*
(curiosity, she ran across the field after it, and fortunately was just in time) Tj T*
(to see it pop down a large rabbit-hole under the hedge.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In another moment down went Alice after it, never once considering how in the) Tj T*
(world she was to get out again.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The rabbit-hole went straight on like a tunnel for some way, and then dipped) Tj T*
(suddenly down, so suddenly that Alice had not a moment to think about stopping) Tj T*
(herself before she found herself falling down a very deep well.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Either the well was very deep, or she fell very slowly, for she had plenty of) Tj T*
(time as she went down to look about her and to wonder what was going to happen) Tj T*
(next. First, she tried to look down and make out what she was coming to, but) Tj T*
(it was too dark to see anything; then she looked at the sides of the well, and) Tj T*
(noticed that they were filled with cupboards and book-shelves; here and there) Tj T*
(she saw maps and pictures hung upon pegs.) Tj T*
